Q: Byte[] to InputStream or OutputStream A: <p>we can convert byte[] array into input stream by using ByteArrayInputStream</p>
<pre><code>String str = &quot;Welcome to awesome Java World&quot;;
byte[] content = str.getBytes();
int size = content.length;
InputStream is = null;
byte[] b = new byte[size];
is = new ByteArrayInputStream(content);
</code></pre>
<p>For full example please check here <a href="http://www.onlinecodegeek.com/2015/09/how-to-convert-byte-into-inputstream.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://www.onlinecodegeek.com/2015/09/how-to-convert-byte-into-inputstream.html</a></p>

Q: How to call a custom widget in a single.php page of wordpress A: <p><strong>Step 1: Create the Widget Class</strong></p>
<p>Add the following to your functions.php:</p>
<pre><code>class CategoryPostInfo_Widget extends WP_Widget {
    public function __construct() {
        parent::__construct(
            'category_post_info_widget',
            'Category Post Info Widget',
            [ 'description' =&gt; 'Shows post info if category matches on single page.' ]
        );
    }



    public function widget($args, $instance) {
        if ( ! is_single() ) return; // Only for single posts

        global $post;

        // Define target category slug (change this to your category slug)
        $target_category = 'news';

        if ( has_category($target_category, $post) ) {
            $image_url = get_the_post_thumbnail_url( $post-&gt;ID, 'medium' );
            $custom_value = get_post_meta( $post-&gt;ID, 'my_custom_field', true ); // Adjust meta key

            echo $args['before_widget'];

            echo '&lt;div class=&quot;category-post-info-widget&quot;&gt;';
            if ( $image_url ) {
                echo '&lt;img src=&quot;' . esc_url($image_url) . '&quot; style=&quot;max-width:100%;&quot; /&gt;';
            }
            if ( $custom_value ) {
                echo '&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Custom Field:&lt;/strong&gt; ' . esc_html($custom_value) . '&lt;/p&gt;';
            }
            echo '&lt;/div&gt;';

            echo $args['after_widget'];
        }
    }

    public function form($instance) {
        echo &quot;&lt;p&gt;This widget doesn't need settings.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;;
    }

    public function update($new_instance, $old_instance) {
        return $new_instance;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Step 2: Register the Widget</strong></p>
<pre><code>add_action('widgets_init', function() {
    register_widget('CategoryPostInfo_Widget');
});

</code></pre>
<p><strong>Step 3: Add the Widget to a Sidebar (Automatically or Manually)</strong></p>
<p>In your sidebar.php (or single.php, or wherever your sidebar lives):</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
if ( is_single() &amp;&amp; has_category('news') ) {
    the_widget('CategoryPostInfo_Widget');
}
?&gt;
</code></pre>
